<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Paris (1763)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1763">1763</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as 1763 Treaty of Paris · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Paris of 1763 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and their allies. France ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and its territory east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> to Britain and lost most of its position in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> treat it as a foundation of British global supremacy, while accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> mark it as the collapse of France's first colonial empire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was signed on 10 February 1763 by the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, with Portugal in agreement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> were not party to it and signed a separate agreement, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-hubertusburg">Treaty of Hubertusburg</a>, five days later. Most conquered territories went back to their former owners. France and Spain restored all their conquests to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Portugal, and Britain returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> to Spain, and Guadeloupe, Martinique, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-lucia">Saint Lucia</a>, Gorée and the Indian trading posts to France.</p><p>In return <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> recognised British sovereignty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominica">Dominica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-vincent-and-the-grenadines">Saint Vincent and the Grenadines</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a>. France ceded the eastern half of French Louisiana, the area from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachian Mountains</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. France kept fishing rights off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> and regained the islands of Saint Pierre and Miquelon. In India France regained its factories but recognised British clients as rulers of key native states and pledged not to send troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Britain agreed to demolish its fortifications in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize">British Honduras</a> but kept a logwood-cutting colony there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had conquered a great deal during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and Guadeloupe to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>. The British debated the relative merits of Guadeloupe, which produced £6 million a year in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, and Canada, which was expensive to keep. They chose Canada for strategic reasons. The prime minister, Lord Bute, wanted a peace that would not push <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> toward a second war.</p><p>The French Foreign Minister, the Duke of Choiseul, expected war to return but needed peace to rebuild. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> preferred its profitable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> islands to the vast Canadian lands, which had been a financial burden. French diplomats believed that without France to keep them in check the American colonists might attempt to revolt. France also asked for toleration of Roman Catholics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> lost all its mainland North American territory except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>. It had already secretly given that land to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the Treaty of Fontainebleau three months earlier, though Spain did not take possession until 1769 and the transfer was not publicly announced until 1764. Article IV allowed the French inhabitants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> eighteen months to emigrate and sold their estates to British subjects, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> agreed to grant liberty of the Catholic religion as far as the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> permit.</p><p>The treaty ended the conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and began an era of British dominance outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> corridor in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> was later reunited under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> in 1803 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adams-onis-treaty">Adams–Onís Treaty</a> in 1819.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Paris (1763)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Seven Years' War (1756–1763), concluded by the <b>Treaty of Paris</b>, stripped France of Canada and its lands in North America east of the Mississippi and confirmed British dominance in India after Plassey (1757).</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>1763 Treaty of Paris</b> forced France to cede Canada and all North American territory east of the Mississippi to Britain, leaving it with Caribbean islands, Saint-Pierre and Miquelon, and five Indian trading posts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The wars of Louis XIV left heavy debts, John Law's paper-money scheme collapsed in 1720, and the Seven Years' War cost France Canada and most of its position in India at the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> of 1763.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>By the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> of 10 February 1763 France ceded Canada and the lands east of the Mississippi to Britain, having already given western Louisiana to Spain in 1762, and kept only the fishing islands of Saint-Pierre and Miquelon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> in 1763 Britain returned Havana to Spain in exchange for Florida.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-occupation-of-havana">British occupation of Havana</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Paris</b> of 1763 returned Manila to Spain, and the British left in April 1764.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-occupation-of-manila">British occupation of Manila</a></li></ul></section>
